<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Transport security for everything Android's own networking stack carries (Task 62, §7).

  ## Cleartext is off, everywhere, in every build

  There is no `debug-overrides` block and no per-domain exception, and that is deliberate.
  A debug override is how a permissive trust configuration reaches production: it is added
  to unblock one afternoon's testing against a self-signed PBX, and nothing ever removes
  it. A deployment that needs to trust its own certificate authority configures one
  explicitly - see StackAccount.customCaPath - which ADDS a trust anchor rather than
  disabling validation.

  ## What this does and does not cover

  It covers Firebase and any HTTP this app makes through the platform.

  It does NOT cover SIP over TLS: PJSIP opens its own sockets and runs its own TLS
  through OpenSSL, so `network_security_config.xml` never sees them. The equivalent
  settings for that path belong to the TLS transport RealPjsipCoreGateway creates, which
  verifies the server certificate against the bundle PjsipTrustStore assembles from the
  device's own trust store. Both halves are needed and neither substitutes for the other;
  docs/security.md says so in one place rather than leaving somebody to discover it. What
  has not happened yet is a TLS registration against a real server - docs/pjsip-migration.md
  P-8 is where that is tracked.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!-- The system store, and nothing else. -->
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
